WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.850
Each task is a ServiceNow-shaped payload.

2
00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:05.453
Its action is create, update or close,

3
00:00:05.453 --> 00:00:10.225
and its de-duplication key is the
provider and the provider's own I D.

4
00:00:10.225 --> 00:00:16.388
Its severity, priority and response window
come from the Ticket-to-Machine finding

5
00:00:16.388 --> 00:00:21.925
contract: P1 immediate, P2 one hour, P3
twenty-four hours, P4 seventy-two hours.

6
00:00:21.925 --> 00:00:25.742
Those windows are program design,
not a federal requirement,

7
00:00:25.742 --> 00:00:29.025
and a test reads the contract
and fails if they drift.

8
00:00:29.025 --> 00:00:32.650
Then the provider's own action
date, kept in a separate field.

9
00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.000
The two dates answer different questions.

10
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.112
The response window says how fast
the owner must pick the task up.

11
00:00:39.112 --> 00:00:42.023
The provider's date says
when the work must be done,

12
00:00:42.023 --> 00:00:44.437
and for a retirement
it may be months away.

13
00:00:44.437 --> 00:00:50.013
Confusing them makes a P3 task look due
in a year, or a retirement due tomorrow.

14
00:00:50.013 --> 00:00:54.699
The payload also carries the contingency
review flag, and the Ticket-to-Machine

15
00:00:54.699 --> 00:00:58.888
block: the Human decision lane, and
the five clauses of the request chain.

16
00:00:58.888 --> 00:01:01.882
Where it came from, who
approves, what to do,

17
00:01:01.882 --> 00:01:04.750
the re-read check, and the evidence kept.
